  <div class="callout warning">
    <p><strong>Funding gap:</strong> Construction of the Weber County extension is not currently funded. Advancing the project ahead of the 2043–2050 plan window would require UDOT and the Legislature to allocate significant funding — likely hundreds of millions of dollars — through federal infrastructure funds, a legislative appropriation, or displacement of other funded projects.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-label jump-target" id="davis-precedent">Analog Comparison</div>
  <h2>Davis County Precedent</h2>

  <p>The Davis County West Davis Corridor provides the nearest historical analog:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 16px 0 20px 24px; line-height: 2.2;">
    <li>Environmental Impact Statement completed: <strong>2017</strong>{% cite "udot-wdc-overview" %}</li>
    <li>Construction funding secured and design finalized: <strong>~2020–2021</strong></li>
    <li>Construction commenced: <strong>Early 2021</strong></li>
    <li>Open to traffic: <strong>January 2024</strong>{% cite "udot-wdc-overview" %}</li>
    <li><strong>Total: approximately 7 years from Environmental Impact Statement completion to opening</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <p>The Davis County project moved from Environmental Impact Statement completion to opening in roughly seven years because funding was secured promptly. The Weber County State Environmental Study is expected to conclude late 2026. If construction funding were secured immediately upon State Environmental Study completion — a scenario not currently supported by the Unified Transportation Plan — an equivalent interval would suggest a possible opening no earlier than the early 2030s. Under the current plan, construction is placed in the 2043–2050 window.</p>

  <div class="section-label jump-target" id="funding-scenarios">Funding Scenarios</div>
  <h2>Realistic Timelines Based on Funding Status</h2>

  <p>The Phase 3 construction timeline depends critically on when (and if) funding is allocated. Below are three scenarios based on the Davis County precedent (approximately 7 years from Environmental Impact Statement completion to opening):</p>

  <table>
    <caption>Phase 3 construction timeline scenarios</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
        <th scope="col">Funding Secured</th>
        <th scope="col">Design Phase</th>
        <th scope="col">Construction Start</th>
        <th scope="col">Est. Opening</th>
        <th scope="col">Current Likelihood</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Accelerated</strong></td>
        <td>Late 2026 / Early 2027</td>
        <td>2027–2029</td>
        <td>2029–2030</td>
        <td>2032–2033</td>
        <td>Very low (no current allocation)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Expedited</strong></td>
        <td>2030</td>
        <td>2030–2032</td>
        <td>2032–2033</td>
        <td>2035–2036</td>
        <td>Low (requires legislative priority)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Official Plan</strong></td>
        <td>2043 (or later)</td>
        <td>2043–2045</td>
        <td>2045–2046</td>
        <td>2048–2050</td>
        <td>Current official timeline</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Property owners should plan for a long timeline. The most likely scenario remains 2043–2050, though local officials are actively lobbying for acceleration. Any property acquisition would not begin until funding is formally allocated and the project enters design phase (at minimum 1–2 years after funding).</p>
